.TH NETMAN 1 "" "netman" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
netman \- network and Bluetooth manager, a NetworkManager and bluez drop-in
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B netman
[\fICOMMAND\fR [\fIARGS\fR]]
.br
.B netman \-tui
.SH DESCRIPTION
Without a command netman prints the status of interfaces, Wi\-Fi, Bluetooth and VPN.
Commands that need root re\-run themselves through sudo (config key privileges.sudo).
A module switched off in the config file is skipped with a message such as \(lqwifi disabled in config\(rq.
This page is generated from the command table (\fBnetman help \-\-man\fR).
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B netman status
interfaces, Wi\-Fi, Bluetooth and VPN at a glance (the default)
.TP
.B netman wifi list
scan and list visible networks (hidden ones as <hidden> + BSSID). Security is shown as OPEN, WEP, WPA, WPA2, WPA3 or 802.1X; the signal as dBm and percent.
.TP
.B netman wifi scan
the same list as TSV for scripts. Columns: FLAG KNOWN SEC DBM PCT BSSID CHAN BAND NBSS HIDDEN NAME. Names are escaped (\exNN for control bytes).
.TP
.B netman wifi info [SSID]
details of a network (default: the connected one). SSID, BSSIDs, security, channel and band, signal, rates, known/autoconnect, last connected.
.TP
.B netman wifi connect SSID [PASSPHRASE] | \-\-eap SSID IDENTITY PASSWORD
join a network; 802.1X with \-\-eap. Without a passphrase a known or open network is joined. A wrong passphrase is reported as such.
.TP
.B netman wifi disconnect
leave the current network
.TP
.B netman wifi forget SSID
remove a saved network (iwd only)
.TP
.B netman wifi wps pbc|pin [PIN]|cancel
WPS push\-button or PIN
.TP
.B netman wifi hotspot start SSID [PASSPHRASE] | stop
share a connection as an access point
.TP
.B netman wifi known [autoconnect SSID on|off]
saved networks, and their autoconnect switch
.TP
.B netman bt list
known and discovered devices
.TP
.B netman bt scan [SECONDS]
discover devices, then list
.TP
.B netman bt info NAME_OR_MAC
details of a device: class, RSSI, battery, profiles
.TP
.B netman bt connect NAME_OR_MAC
connect a paired device
.TP
.B netman bt disconnect NAME_OR_MAC
disconnect a device
.TP
.B netman bt pair NAME_OR_MAC [\-\-pin PIN]
pair a device (\-\-pin answers every prompt)
.TP
.B netman bt trust NAME_OR_MAC
trust a device
.TP
.B netman bt remove NAME_OR_MAC
forget a device
.TP
.B netman bt power on|off
switch the adapter
.TP
.B netman bt agent [SECONDS]
run the pairing agent (PIN/passkey prompts on this terminal)
.TP
.B netman net list
interfaces with addresses, gateways, routes
.TP
.B netman net show IFACE
one interface with link speed and live rx/tx rate
.TP
.B netman net up IFACE
set the link up
.TP
.B netman net down IFACE
set the link down
.TP
.B netman net set IFACE ADDR/LEN [GATEWAY]
static address (and default gateway)
.TP
.B netman net dhcp IFACE
get a lease with the built\-in DHCP client and apply it. Needs net.dhcp = yes.
.TP
.B netman net dns IFACE NS... | IFACE clear
set or clear DNS servers. Where they go is config net.dns_mode.
.TP
.B netman net profile list
saved profiles (/etc/netman/NAME.conf)
.TP
.B netman net profile save NAME IFACE dhcp|ADDR/LEN [GATEWAY [DNS...]]
create or replace a profile
.TP
.B netman net profile apply NAME
bring a profile up
.TP
.B netman roam status
probe setting and the score of each live interface
.TP
.B netman roam prefer PROFILE PRIORITY
set a profile priority (higher wins)
.TP
.B netman tor up
start Tor and the fail\-closed firewall (transparent mode: all TCP + DNS via Tor, everything else dropped). Needs tor installed. Rules go in BEFORE tor starts and stay until `tor down`. Threat model: see the readme; Tor does not anonymise logged\-in accounts.
.TP
.B netman tor down
stop Tor, then remove the firewall table
.TP
.B netman tor status
mode, bootstrap %, circuits, exit ip, bridges, hidden services, SOCKS settings
.TP
.B netman tor newnym
new Tor identity: fresh circuits for new connections
.TP
.B netman tor bridge add LINE | clear
set or clear obfs4 / snowflake bridges. LINE e.g. 'obfs4 1.2.3.4:443 FINGERPRINT cert=... iat\-mode=0'.
.TP
.B netman tor exit CC|off
force the exit country (strict)
.TP
.B netman tor hs add NAME PORT HOST:PORT | list | del NAME
onion services: publish a local port as a .onion
.TP
.B netman vpn list
VPN profiles (WireGuard, OpenVPN, IPsec/IKEv2, L2TP, OpenConnect) and their state
.TP
.B netman ssh status
SSH profiles and connections: state, user@host:port, reconnects. Rows: NAME up|connecting|down user@host:port. The same list is the SSH tab.
.TP
.B netman ssh save NAME host=H [user=U key=PATH jump=J1,J2 port=N forward=L:... keepalive=N control=yes reconnect=yes tun=L:R strict=yes|accept\-new]
create or change an SSH profile. Forwards: forward=L:8080:db:5432 (local), R:9000:localhost:22 (remote), D:1080 (SOCKS); repeat forward= up to 8 times. strict accepts only yes or accept\-new: netman never turns host key checking off, and a profile that asks for it is refused. Stored as NAME.ssh.
.TP
.B netman ssh show NAME
print a profile
.TP
.B netman ssh argv NAME
print the exact ssh command line a profile runs (nothing is started)
.TP
.B netman ssh up NAME
start the connection in the background; it reconnects with backoff when it drops. A supervisor keeps ssh \-N running, logs to the state dir, and assigns tun_addr once an ssh \-w device exists.
.TP
.B netman ssh down NAME
stop the connection and its supervisor
.TP
.B netman ssh apply
start every profile with auto = yes
.TP
.B netman ssh hostkey NAME
fetch and show the host key fingerprint (ssh\-keyscan + ssh\-keygen \-lf). Compare it with what the server's admin gave you, then `ssh trust`.
.TP
.B netman ssh trust NAME SHA256:FP [HOST]
store the host key only if its fingerprint equals FP. Safer than accept\-new: the key goes into the known_hosts file only when the fingerprint you typed matches what the host presents.
.TP
.B netman ssh check NAME
is the ControlMaster alive (ssh \-O check)
.TP
.B netman ssh exec NAME COMMAND...
run a command over the profile's ControlMaster
.TP
.B netman ssh del NAME
delete a profile (stop it first)
.TP
.B netman mesh status
mesh profiles: state, kind, device, peers handshaked/total. The same list is the Mesh tab.
.TP
.B netman mesh save NAME kind=batman ifaces=A,B | kind=wg self=NODE [k=v...]
create or change a mesh profile. batman: ifaces orig_interval gw_mode hop_penalty mtu addr. wg: self port prefix keepalive key addr. Stored as NAME.mesh.
.TP
.B netman mesh show NAME
print a profile
.TP
.B netman mesh init NAME
generate this node's WireGuard private key (mode 0600) and print the public key; never overwrites
.TP
.B netman mesh node NAME add NODE PUBKEY ENDPOINT|\- ADDRESS | del NODE | list
edit the WireGuard mesh peer list. One list for every node. ENDPOINT \- means the node is not reachable directly (it must connect to you).
.TP
.B netman mesh gen NAME [NODE]
print the wg\-quick config of a node (default: this one). Every other node becomes a peer with AllowedIPs = its mesh address only. The private key is filled in only for this node.
.TP
.B netman mesh up NAME
bring the mesh up (batman: ip link add type batadv; wg: netlink, no wg binary)
.TP
.B netman mesh down NAME
remove the mesh device
.TP
.B netman mesh apply
bring up every mesh profile with auto = yes
.TP
.B netman mesh neighbors NAME
batman\-adv neighbours (needs batctl)
.TP
.B netman mesh del NAME
delete a profile (the peer list and key file are kept)
.TP
.B netman bond status
bonds: state, mode, slaves up/total, active slave. The same list is the Bond tab.
.TP
.B netman bond modes
the bonding modes and what each needs from the switch
.TP
.B netman bond show NAME
live state and profile of a bond
.TP
.B netman bond add NAME [KEY=VALUE...] SLAVE...
create a bond and enslave interfaces. e.g. `bond add bond0 mode=802.3ad miimon=100 eth0 eth1`. Shares the code and NAME.ent profiles of `ent save NAME bond`.
.TP
.B netman bond del NAME
delete the bond
.TP
.B netman bond slave add|del NAME IFACE
add or remove one slave of a bond
.TP
.B netman bond apply
re\-create every saved bond
.TP
.B netman tunnel status
tunnels: state, kind, local>remote, mtu, rx/tx. The same list is the Tunnel tab.
.TP
.B netman tunnel save NAME kind=gre|gretap|ipip|sit|vxlan|geneve|l2tp local=A remote=B [k=v...]
create or change a tunnel profile. Keys: link addr key ttl mtu auto vni port group, l2tp: tunnel_id peer_tunnel_id session_id peer_session_id encap lport rport. mtu = auto subtracts the encapsulation overhead from the underlay MTU. Stored as NAME.tun.
.TP
.B netman tunnel show NAME
print a profile
.TP
.B netman tunnel mtu NAME
show the underlay MTU, the overhead and the tunnel MTU `auto` picks
.TP
.B netman tunnel up NAME
create the tunnel device, set mtu and address, bring it up
.TP
.B netman tunnel down NAME
delete the tunnel device
.TP
.B netman tunnel apply
bring up every tunnel with auto = yes
.TP
.B netman tunnel del NAME
delete a profile
.TP
.B netman vpn import FILE [NAME]
import a VPN profile: WireGuard / OpenVPN / NetworkManager .nmconnection / strongSwan conf / netman .vpn (into /etc/netman/vpn, mode 0600). Refuses to overwrite. Converts NetworkManager keyfiles (wireguard, openvpn, strongswan, l2tp, openconnect) and strongSwan swanctl.conf / ipsec.conf; PPTP is refused (insecure). Passwords kept in secret stores are not imported; the note says what was left out. Directory override: env NETMAN_VPN_CONF_DIR.
.TP
.B netman vpn info NAME
everything known about one profile: endpoint, handshake, transfer, allowed ips, split or full tunnel, kill switch
.TP
.B netman vpn up NAME [WAIT_S]
bring a tunnel up (plus the kill switch if vpn.killswitch)
.TP
.B netman vpn down NAME
take a tunnel down
.TP
.B netman vpn status [NAME]
tunnel state, handshake, transfer
.TP
.B netman vpn run NAME COMMAND...
run one command through the tunnel in its own namespace
.TP
.B netman vpn killswitch NAME on|off
nftables kill switch for a tunnel
.TP
.B netman vpn providers
supported providers (Mullvad, NordVPN)
.TP
.B netman vpn relays PROVIDER [COUNTRY]
list a provider's relays (fetches with curl)
.TP
.B netman vpn connect PROVIDER PROFILE [COUNTRY [CITY]] [\-\-fastest] [\-\-via COUNTRY]
write a profile for a provider relay
.TP
.B netman fw show
the ruleset netman owns, as loaded
.TP
.B netman fw plan [PRESET]
print the ruleset that would be applied
.TP
.B netman fw apply [PRESET]
apply atomically; rolls back unless confirmed. Needs firewall.enabled = yes. firewall.confirm_s sets the window.
.TP
.B netman fw diff [PRESET]
compare the plan with what was last applied
.TP
.B netman fw confirm
keep the applied rules
.TP
.B netman fw rollback
restore the previous rules now
.TP
.B netman fw preset workstation|lockdown
set the default preset (config firewall.preset)
.TP
.B netman metrics show
per\-interface rates and totals
.TP
.B netman metrics record
append one sample per interface to metrics.dir
.TP
.B netman metrics watch [COUNT]
print rates every metrics.interval_s seconds
.TP
.B netman ent list
saved enterprise profiles (bond, bridge, vlan, route, ecmp, tether ...)
.TP
.B netman ent show NAME
live state of a profile's device
.TP
.B netman ent save NAME KIND [KEY=VALUE...]
create or replace a profile. KIND: bond team bridge vlan macvlan ipvlan veth dummy tether dot1x route rule ecmp link ipv6 wol. Keys are the profile keys, e.g. `ent save bond0 bond mode=802.3ad member=eth0 member=eth1 miimon=100`. Stored as NAME.ent in the netman config directory.
.TP
.B netman ent add NAME
create the device / install the routes of a profile. Needs enterprise.enabled = yes. Everything goes through rtnetlink, ethtool ioctls and /proc/sys; only nft, dnsmasq and wpa_supplicant are ever exec'd, and only for tether and 802.1X.
.TP
.B netman ent del NAME
tear a profile's device / routes down
.TP
.B netman ent apply
apply every saved profile in dependency order. dummy/veth, bond/team, bridge, vlan/macvlan/ipvlan, link/ipv6/wol, route/rule/ecmp, tether/dot1x.
.TP
.B netman ent ecmp NAME
run the multi\-uplink failover loop for an ecmp profile. Probes each uplink with an ICMP echo bound to its interface; fail_after / ok_after consecutive results flip it, the default route (metric 50) follows. Ctrl\-C stops.
.TP
.B netman ent tether
detected USB (RNDIS/NCM/ECM/ipheth) and Bluetooth PAN tethering devices
.TP
.B netman wol IFACE MAC
send a Wake\-on\-LAN magic packet out of IFACE. UDP broadcast to port 9. Setting the NIC itself to wake on LAN is `ent save NAME wol wol=g`.
.TP
.B netman licence status
licences and their expiry (exit 1 when any expires soon or has). Files NAME.lic in enterprise.licence_dir. Warns inside enterprise.licence_warn_days.
.TP
.B netman licence renew NAME
renew a licence through enterprise.renew_hook. The hook gets NAME CURRENT_PATH NEW_PATH and writes the new licence; netman checks it names the same licence and expires later, then swaps atomically and keeps NAME.lic.prev. netman itself never contacts a network.
.TP
.B netman key rotate NAME
new WireGuard key for NAME; prints the new public key. Writes NAME.key mode 0600, keeps the old one as NAME.key.prev, then runs enterprise.key_apply_hook (NAME PUBKEY); if the hook fails the old key is restored. No `wg` binary needed.
.TP
.B netman key show NAME
public key of NAME.key
.TP
.B netman wg show [IF]
live WireGuard state: port, peers, endpoint, allowed ips, handshake age, transfer. Without IF: every profile of the vpn directory that is up. Reading the device needs root.
.TP
.B netman wg genkey
new private key on stdout (nothing is written)
.TP
.B netman wg pubkey
public key for the private key on stdin
.TP
.B netman wg genpsk
new preshared key on stdout
.TP
.B netman wg peer add IF PUBKEY [allowed=IP,..] [endpoint=HOST:PORT] [keepalive=S] [psk\-file=FILE]
add or update ONE peer on a running interface; the other peers and their sessions are not touched. Allowed ips are written as given (host bits masked). Routes are not changed: a peer inside the interface's own subnet needs none, anything else is a route you add (or put in the profile). A key that already exists has its allowed ips, endpoint and keepalive replaced.
.TP
.B netman wg peer del IF PUBKEY
remove one peer from a running interface. Exit 1 when the interface has no such peer.
.TP
.B netman wg peer endpoint IF PUBKEY HOST:PORT
point an existing peer at a new endpoint (roaming server, new IP) without restarting. Never creates a peer: an unknown key is an error.
.TP
.B netman config show
every setting with its value and where it came from
.TP
.B netman config check
validate the config files and environment. Errors are FILE:LINE with a did\-you\-mean for unknown keys.
.TP
.B netman config set SECTION.KEY VALUE
edit the user config file (atomic, comments kept)
.TP
.B netman settings
the settings panel's data as a list: key, value, where it comes from, what it does (= settings list). The TUI panel is key S inside `netman \-tui`. Everything below works on the same schema (src/settings.c).
.TP
.B netman settings list [SECTION|TAB] [\-\-all]
every setting: key, value, source (default/system/user/env), description. Settings of a module that is switched off are left out; \-\-all lists them. [restart] = needs a restart of the thing that reads it, [root] = acting on it needs root.
.TP
.B netman settings get KEY
print the effective value of one setting
.TP
.B netman settings set KEY VALUE
validate and write one setting into the user file (atomic, comments kept). Same as config set, plus a note when the change needs a restart or an environment variable overrides it.
.TP
.B netman settings toggle KEY
flip a yes/no setting
.TP
.B netman settings reset KEY
remove the setting from the user file (back to the system file or the default)
.TP
.B netman settings check [\-\-json]
validate the config files and environment; \-\-json for scripts. Exit 1 with FILE:LINE when something is wrong. Lists the settings an environment variable overrides.
.TP
.B netman settings schema
the schema in the pipe format of desktop's settings.schema (key|type|extra|apply|default|label)
.TP
.B netman settings \-\-dump\-api
JSON: the whole schema, the current values and where each one comes from. For a launcher or panel that hosts netman's settings: tabs, then one object per setting with type, range or choices, default, value, source, needs_root, restart, gate and hidden.
.TP
.B netman settings menu [SECTION|TAB] [\-\-all]
dmenu\-ready lines: `section.key = value  # description`. netman settings menu | dmenu \-l 20 | netman settings pick
.TP
.B netman settings pick [\-\-choices] [\-\-value VALUE]
read the chosen menu line on stdin, offer the values in a menu and set the one chosen. The value menu is $NETMAN_MENU (a shell command, default `dmenu \-p "$NETMAN_MENU_PROMPT"`); nothing chosen = nothing changed. \-\-choices prints the choices instead; \-\-value V sets V without asking.
.TP
.B netman daemon [\-\-bus ADDRESS]
serve the NetworkManager D\-Bus API (drop\-in for NM clients). Claims org.freedesktop.NetworkManager on the system bus (fails if a NetworkManager owns it) and answers until SIGTERM. \-\-bus selects another bus, e.g. a private dbus\-daemon. See docs/nm\-api\-surface.md for what is implemented.
.TP
.B netman config toggle SECTION.KEY
flip a yes/no setting
.TP
.B netman config example
print the documented example config
.TP
.B netman help [TOPIC]
this list, or the help for one command or group
.TP
.B netman \-tui
the terminal UI (also \-\-tui, \-t); ? inside shows help
.SH COMMAND GROUPS
.TP
.B ent
enterprise networking: bonding/teaming, bridges with STP and vlan filtering, vlans and QinQ, macvlan/ipvlan, veth, routes, rules and tables, ECMP with failover, link settings, IPv6 prefix delegation, USB/Bluetooth tethering, wired 802.1X. Profiles are NAME.ent files ("key = value", same syntax as net profiles) in the netman config directory. team is bonding: the kernel team driver is deprecated, so kind=team maps runner= (activebackup, lacp, loadbalance, broadcast, roundrobin) onto the bonding driver. Tethering share\-out uses its own nft table `inet netman_tether` (firewall.h replaces `inet netman`, so the two never clobber each other); an accept there cannot override a drop in another table, so a strict firewall must allow the downstream interface itself. Changing commands need enterprise.enabled = yes.
.TP
.B settings
the settings schema (src/settings.c) as commands: list, get, set, toggle, reset, check, a JSON API (\-\-dump\-api) and dmenu helpers (menu | dmenu | pick). The TUI panel on key S shows the same data. Writes go to ~/.config/netman/netman.conf only, atomically, comments kept.
.TP
.B licence
licence files NAME.lic with expiry, the expiry check (exit 1 when anything expires soon), and hook\-driven renewal. Switched by licence.enabled.
.TP
.B key
WireGuard key rotation (NAME.key in enterprise.licence_dir). Switched by licence.enabled.
.TP
.B wol
Wake\-on\-LAN magic packet. Switched by enterprise.enabled.
.TP
.B wg
native WireGuard over generic netlink (src/mods/wireguard.c), no wg binary: keys and live peer edits on a running interface (add, delete, new endpoint: the other peers keep their sessions). Profiles and routes stay with the vpn commands. Switched by wireguard.enabled; with it off only OpenVPN profiles come up.
.SH TUI
Start it with \fBnetman \-tui\fR; \fB?\fR inside shows the same text:
.nf
GENERAL
  tab / shift\-tab, h l, 1\-5   switch tab (tabs of modules disabled in config are hidden)
  j k, arrows, PgUp PgDn      move;  g G  first / last
  /                           filter by name
  r                           refresh / rescan
  S                           settings panel (see SETTINGS below)
  i                           details popup for the selected row;  I  toggle the big "all info" view
                              (replaces the list; j k pick another row, PgUp PgDn scroll, esc / I close)
  ?                           this help;  q  quit
WI\-FI
  enter    connect: known networks join at once; others ask for the passphrase
           (masked; tab shows/hides it); open networks ask for confirmation;
           802.1X asks identity then password; a wrong password says so and offers a retry
  i        details: BSSIDs, security (pairwise / group / AKM), WPS, channel/width, signal, noise, rx/tx bitrate,
           first/last seen, roam history, IP/gateway/DNS/lease, link speed, rx/tx;  I  the same plus the raw backend text
  s        cycle sort: signal / name / secured first / open first;  o  all / only open / only secured
  d x a    disconnect / forget / toggle autoconnect;  w  WPS;  H  hotspot
  Signal is dBm and percent (green good, yellow ok, red weak); a lock marks security,
  OPEN networks get a warning colour. Without Unicode: [*] and open.
BLUETOOTH
  enter conn/disc   p pair   t trust   x remove   s scan   o power   i details   I all info (adds the adapter:
  powered, discoverable, pairable, paired/connected counts)
ETHERNET / IP
  addresses, gateway, DNS, lease, link speed and live rx/tx per interface;  i  details: driver, MAC, MTU,
  speed/duplex, link detected, IPv4/IPv6, DHCP server and lease times, rx/tx rates and errors;  I  adds sysfs
VPN
  Profiles are <dir>/NAME.conf (WireGuard) or NAME.ovpn; dir = /etc/netman/vpn or env NETMAN_VPN_CONF_DIR.
  An empty list names the directory; add a profile with  netman vpn import FILE [NAME].
  enter    up / down the selected profile (also turns the kill switch on when vpn.killswitch = yes)
  K        toggle the nftables kill switch;  i  details: endpoint, allowed ips, handshake age, transfer, split tunnel
  States: up, down, connecting, failed; locked = unreadable (run as root), untrusted = file must be root/self
  owned and not group/world writable. Creating tunnels needs root: with VPN profiles present the TUI asks sudo.
  r        refresh
ENTERPRISE (only with enterprise.enabled = yes)
  read\-only status: bonds, bridges, vlans, uplinks (ECMP), tethering (when tether.enabled) and licences
  (when licence.enabled), from the saved .ent profiles and the live kernel state;  i  details of the selected
  row;  I  the same plus the raw profile text. Changes are made with  netman ent ... / licence ... / key ...
SETTINGS (key S: every netman.conf key, same data as `netman settings`)
  tabs: Modules (the on/off switch of every module) then one tab per area; a module's tab exists only while it is enabled
  j k move;  h l, tab, 1\-9 change tab;  / search all settings;  esc clears the search, then closes;  S or q close
  enter / space   toggle a yes/no, cycle a choice, edit text and numbers (enter saves, esc cancels, errors show as you type)
  + \-  step a number or cycle a choice backwards;  e  edit any value as text;  d  back to the default (removes the line)
  mouse: click a tab or a row, click the selected row again to change it, wheel scrolls
  Each change is saved at once to ~/.config/netman/netman.conf (atomic, comments kept) and applied live, except rows
  marked "restart needed" (a daemon or tor process reads them at start). The right column and the line under the list
  say where the value comes from (default, system /etc, user, env = an environment variable that overrides the file).
HEADER (row 2, every tab)
  wifi / bt status, hostname, default route (iface via gateway), ip (pub = a public address), dns,
  and the network score 0\-100 with its reasons (rtt, loss, dBm). The score is measured in the background
  (3 pings to the gateway); "ping n/a" = the kernel does not allow unprivileged ICMP. A field the system
  does not report is shown as \-. On a narrow terminal the lowest\-priority fields are dropped.
.fi
.SH CONFIGURATION
Settings come from defaults, \fI/etc/netman/netman.conf\fR, \fI~/.config/netman/netman.conf\fR and the environment variables \fBNETMAN_\fISECTION\fB_\fIKEY\fR, later ones win. \fBnetman config show\fR lists every value and where it came from; \fBnetman config check\fR validates; \fBnetman config set\fR and \fBtoggle\fR edit the user file.
.TP
.B wifi.enabled
Wi\-Fi support: the wifi commands, the Wi\-Fi tab. no = skipped everywhere (CLI says 'wifi disabled in config'). Default: yes. Panel tab: Wi\-Fi.
.TP
.B wifi.backend
Which daemon to talk to. auto = wpa_supplicant if its control socket exists for the interface, else iwd. Default: auto. Panel tab: Wi\-Fi.
.TP
.B wifi.autoconnect
The daemon joins known networks by itself (per\-network switch: wifi known autoconnect SSID on|off). Default: yes. Panel tab: Wi\-Fi.
.TP
.B wifi.rssi_good
dBm at or above which a signal is 'good' (green in the TUI, 4 quality steps). Default: \-55. Panel tab: Wi\-Fi.
.TP
.B wifi.rssi_ok
dBm at or above which a signal is 'ok'. Must be lower than rssi_good. Default: \-65. Panel tab: Wi\-Fi.
.TP
.B wifi.rssi_weak
dBm at or above which a signal is 'weak' (below it: bad). Must be lower than rssi_ok. Default: \-75. Panel tab: Wi\-Fi.
.TP
.B bluetooth.enabled
Bluetooth support: the bt commands, the Bluetooth tab. Default: yes. Panel tab: Bluetooth.
.TP
.B bluetooth.agent
Register netman's own pairing agent (PIN / passkey / confirmation prompts). no = rely on another agent such as your desktop's. Default: yes. Panel tab: Bluetooth.
.TP
.B net.enabled
Ethernet / IP management: the net and roam commands, the Ethernet/IP tab. Default: yes. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: Network.
.TP
.B net.dhcp
Built\-in DHCPv4 client. no = `net dhcp` is refused and dhcp profiles are not applied (static profiles only). Default: yes. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: Network.
.TP
.B net.dns_mode
Where DNS servers go: resolved = systemd\-resolved, resolvconf = write /etc/resolv.conf, auto = resolved when it runs. Default: auto. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: Network.
.TP
.B net.state_dir
Lease cache and the per\-machine MAC secret. Default: /var/lib/netman. Restart needed. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: Network.
.TP
.B roaming.enabled
Autoconnect policy and the `roam` commands. Default: yes. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: Network.
.TP
.B roaming.probe
Check connectivity after a network comes up (needs probe_url); a captive portal or no internet counts as a failure. Default: yes. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: Network.
.TP
.B roaming.probe_url
Plain\-http URL fetched by the probe: 204 = internet, any other answer = captive portal. Empty = no probing. Default: http://connectivitycheck.gstatic.com/generate_204. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: Network.
.TP
.B roaming.random_mac
Use a stable random (locally administered) MAC per profile instead of the hardware address. Default: no. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: Network.
.TP
.B roaming.score_threshold
A network scoring below this (0..100: signal, gateway RTT, loss, captive portal) is dropped for another candidate. Default: 20. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: Network.
.TP
.B vpn.enabled
WireGuard / OpenVPN / provider support: the vpn commands, the VPN tab. Default: yes. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: VPN.
.TP
.B vpn.killswitch
`vpn up` also turns the nftables kill switch on (traffic only through the tunnel). Default: no. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: VPN.
.TP
.B vpn.watchdog
The daemon reconnects a tunnel that died or whose handshake went stale. Default: yes. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: VPN.
.TP
.B vpn.openvpn
OpenVPN profiles (NAME.ovpn). no = `vpn up` refuses them. Default: yes. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: VPN.
.TP
.B vpn.ipsec
IPsec / IKEv2 profiles (Type = ipsec, strongSwan swanctl). no = `vpn up` refuses them. Default: yes. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: VPN.
.TP
.B vpn.l2tp
L2TP/IPsec profiles (Type = l2tp, xl2tpd + pppd). no = `vpn up` refuses them. Default: yes. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: VPN.
.TP
.B vpn.openconnect
OpenConnect profiles: AnyConnect, GlobalProtect, Pulse, ... (Type = openconnect). no = `vpn up` refuses them. Default: yes. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: VPN.
.TP
.B vpn.sstp
SSTP profiles are recognised but not supported (no maintained client): this only changes the message. Default: yes. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: VPN.
.TP
.B firewall.enabled
Firewall management. no = fw apply / confirm / rollback are refused (show, plan, diff and preset still work). Off by default: it changes your rules. Default: no. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: Firewall.
.TP
.B firewall.preset
Ruleset `fw plan` and `fw apply` start from when you name no preset. Default: workstation. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: Firewall.
.TP
.B firewall.confirm_s
fw apply rolls back by itself unless you run `fw confirm` within this many seconds. 0 = apply for good at once. Default: 30. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: Firewall.
.TP
.B firewall.default_zone
Zone of interfaces that have none: trusted = accept all, home, public (stealth + limits), untrusted (nothing in). Default: public. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: Firewall.
.TP
.B metrics.enabled
Interface counters and rates: the metrics commands, the rates in the TUI. Default: yes. Panel tab: Metrics.
.TP
.B metrics.dir
Where `metrics record` appends one TSV file per interface (rotated at 1 MiB). Default: /var/lib/netman/metrics. Panel tab: Metrics.
.TP
.B metrics.interval_s
Seconds between samples for `metrics watch` and `metrics record \-\-loop`. Default: 5. Panel tab: Metrics.
.TP
.B enterprise.enabled
Enterprise networking: bonding, bridges, vlans, routes and rules, ECMP, tethering. Off by default: it changes links and routes. Default: no. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: Enterprise.
.TP
.B enterprise.probe_ms
ECMP: per\-uplink ICMP probe timeout and the tick between probes (ms). Default: 1000. Restart needed. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: Enterprise.
.TP
.B enterprise.licence_dir
Where licence (.lic) and VPN key (.key) files live. Default: /etc/netman/licences. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: Enterprise.
.TP
.B enterprise.licence_warn_days
`licence status` flags a licence expiring within this many days. Default: 30. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: Enterprise.
.TP
.B enterprise.renew_hook
Command (split on spaces, no shell) run by `licence renew NAME`: gets NAME CURRENT_PATH NEW_PATH and must write the renewed licence to NEW_PATH. netman never contacts a network for renewal itself. Default: . Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: Enterprise.
.TP
.B enterprise.key_apply_hook
Command run after `key rotate NAME`: gets NAME NEW_PUBKEY; failing restores the old key. Default: . Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: Enterprise.
.TP
.B licence.enabled
Licence files, expiry check, renewal hook and WireGuard key rotation: the licence and key commands, the licence line in the Enterprise tab. Default: yes. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: Enterprise.
.TP
.B tether.enabled
USB / Bluetooth tethering detection and share\-out: `ent tether`, tether profile adds, the tether rows in the Enterprise tab. Off by default: sharing changes firewall and forwarding. Default: no. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: Enterprise.
.TP
.B wireguard.enabled
Native WireGuard (generic netlink): the wg commands and WireGuard profiles of the vpn commands. no = only OpenVPN profiles work. Default: yes. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: VPN.
.TP
.B nmapi.enabled
NetworkManager D\-Bus API (org.freedesktop.NetworkManager) served by `netman daemon`, so nm\-applet / libnm / nmcli style clients work against netman. Default: yes. Restart needed. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: Daemon.
.TP
.B nmapi.refresh_s
Seconds between the daemon's looks at the links and the Wi\-Fi state (it emits the D\-Bus signals for whatever changed). Default: 5. Restart needed. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: Daemon.
.TP
.B tor.enabled
Tor routing (`netman tor ...`, the Tor tab). Off by default: it loads a fail\-closed firewall table and runs a tor process. Needs tor installed. Default: no. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: Tor.
.TP
.B tor.mode
transparent: nftables sends the routed users' TCP and DNS through Tor and DROPS everything else (no leaks). socks: only prints the SOCKS address; apps that ignore it are not protected. Default: transparent. Restart needed. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: Tor.
.TP
.B tor.socks_port
Tor SocksPort on 127.0.0.1 (per\-app mode). Default: 9050. Restart needed. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: Tor.
.TP
.B tor.trans_port
Tor TransPort on 127.0.0.1 (transparent mode). Default: 9040. Restart needed. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: Tor.
.TP
.B tor.dns_port
Tor DNSPort on 127.0.0.1 (transparent mode). Default: 5353. Restart needed. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: Tor.
.TP
.B tor.control_port
Tor ControlPort on 127.0.0.1. 0 = a unix socket in tor.dir (default, safer). Cookie authentication either way. Default: 0. Restart needed. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: Tor.
.TP
.B tor.dir
Where the generated torrc, data directory, cookie and hidden\-service keys live (tmpfs by default). Default: /run/netman/tor. Restart needed. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: Tor.
.TP
.B tor.profile
Persisted bridges, exit country and hidden services (what `netman tor bridge|exit|hs` edit). Default: /etc/netman/tor.conf. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: Tor.
.TP
.B tor.user
Account tor runs as. Its traffic is the only one the firewall lets out. Default: tor. Restart needed. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: Tor.
.TP
.B tor.route_uids
Numeric uids forced through Tor, comma separated. Empty = everybody but tor's own user. Default: . Restart needed. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: Tor.
.TP
.B tor.route_ifaces
Interfaces whose forwarded traffic (hotspot, router) is also forced through Tor, e.g. wlan1. Empty = none. Default: . Restart needed. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: Tor.
.TP
.B tor.obfs4_bin
obfs4 pluggable transport binary, used when an obfs4 bridge is set. Default: /usr/bin/obfs4proxy. Restart needed. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: Tor.
.TP
.B tor.snowflake_bin
Snowflake pluggable transport binary, used when a snowflake bridge is set. Default: /usr/bin/snowflake\-client. Restart needed. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: Tor.
.TP
.B ssh.enabled
SSH profiles and tunnels (`netman ssh ...`, the SSH tab). Off by default. Runs your ssh binary; host key checking is never turned off. Default: no. Panel tab: SSH.
.TP
.B ssh.dir
Profiles NAME.ssh (host, user, key, jump hosts, forwards, keepalive, reconnect). Default: /etc/netman/ssh. Panel tab: SSH.
.TP
.B ssh.state_dir
Pid, state, log and ControlMaster sockets of running connections. Default: /run/netman/ssh. Restart needed. Panel tab: SSH.
.TP
.B ssh.known_hosts
known_hosts file netman uses (UserKnownHostsFile). Fill it with `netman ssh trust NAME SHA256:FINGERPRINT`. Default: /etc/netman/ssh/known_hosts. Panel tab: SSH.
.TP
.B ssh.ssh_bin
Absolute path of the ssh client. Empty = ssh from PATH. Default: . Panel tab: SSH.
.TP
.B ssh.keyscan_bin
Absolute path of ssh\-keyscan, used by `ssh hostkey`. Empty = from PATH. Default: . Panel tab: SSH.
.TP
.B ssh.keygen_bin
Absolute path of ssh\-keygen, used for fingerprints (\-lf). Empty = from PATH. Default: . Panel tab: SSH.
.TP
.B mesh.enabled
Mesh networks (`netman mesh ...`, the Mesh tab): batman\-adv and a WireGuard full mesh. Off by default: it creates links. Default: no. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: Mesh.
.TP
.B mesh.dir
Profiles NAME.mesh, peer lists NAME.peers and private keys NAME.key (mode 0600). Default: /etc/netman/mesh. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: Mesh.
.TP
.B mesh.ip_bin
Absolute path of ip (iproute2), needed for batman\-adv only; the WireGuard mesh uses netlink. Empty = from PATH. Default: . Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: Mesh.
.TP
.B mesh.batctl_bin
Absolute path of batctl, optional: tuning and `mesh neighbors` for batman\-adv. Empty = from PATH. Default: . Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: Mesh.
.TP
.B bonding.enabled
Link bonding (`netman bond ...`, the Bond tab). Off by default: it creates links. Same code as the enterprise bond profiles. Default: no. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: Bond.
.TP
.B tunnel.enabled
GRE / gretap / ipip / sit / VXLAN / Geneve / L2TPv3 tunnels (`netman tunnel ...`, the Tunnel tab). Off by default: it creates links. Default: no. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: Tunnel.
.TP
.B tunnel.dir
Tunnel profiles NAME.tun. Default: /etc/netman/tunnel. Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: Tunnel.
.TP
.B tunnel.ip_bin
Absolute path of ip (iproute2), used for L2TPv3 only; the other kinds use netlink. Empty = from PATH. Default: . Acting on it needs root. Panel tab: Tunnel.
.TP
.B tui.theme
TUI colours. Default: default. Panel tab: Interface.
.TP
.B tui.unicode
Box drawing and glyphs. auto = by locale; no = plain ASCII. Default: auto. Panel tab: Interface.
.TP
.B privileges.sudo
When netman re\-runs itself through sudo: auto = only when the command needs root, never, always. Default: auto. Panel tab: Interface.
.SH FILES
.I /etc/netman/netman.conf
.br
.I ~/.config/netman/netman.conf
.br
.I /etc/netman/NAME.conf
(network profiles)
